# Reproduce: cargo test -p xai-grok-update --offline --locked
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, rustc 1.94.0 (4a4ef493e), no network access used by the tests.
# Commit under test: the commit that adds these tests and this transcript together (previous HEAD 88903bf7).
# Scope: the auto-update download-and-activate pipeline in crates/codegen/xai-grok-update, driven against a self-hosted release feed on 127.0.0.1.

== what the new suite drives

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-update/tests/test_update_feed_e2e.rs` calls the shipped
`auto_update::run_update` -- the same entry point `chaos update` reaches -- against a
feed made of two loopback servers:

- a wiremock server answering `GET /repos/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/latest`,
  reached through the production override `CHAOS_GH_API_BASE`;
- `tests/common/artifact_server.rs`, a plain `TcpListener` that serves the release
  asset bytes and the `.sig` next to them, reached through `CHAOS_GH_DOWNLOAD_BASE`.
  wiremock cannot be used for the asset bytes because it buffers a whole response
  body, and the truncating mode that the interrupted-download case needs has to cut a
  stream that is already in flight.

Before any update is attempted, `assert_feed_is_loopback()` re-reads both variables and
panics unless both resolve to `127.0.0.1`. That check exists because an earlier draft of
this file dropped the API guard on return, which cleared `CHAOS_GH_API_BASE`, and the
test then went and installed a real release (0.2.5 -> 0.4.2) off github.com while
believing it was talking to itself. `reset_home()` in `tests/common/mod.rs` now also
removes both variables so a guard dropped late cannot re-point the next test at a
dead server.

Each case asserts on state the process actually left behind: which build `bin/chaos`
resolves to, whether the served artifact was executed (the artifacts are `/bin/sh`
scripts that append to a marker file, so the count is a fact about exec, not about a log
line), which bytes are in the versioned file under `downloads/`, and whether the user's
`config.toml` and `sessions/` survived.

== the six cases, and what each one pins

  running 6 tests
  test result: ok. 6 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored

1. `an_upgrade_activates_the_feed_artifact_and_keeps_user_state`
   A 0.2.5 install updates to the 0.2.7 the feed advertises. Exactly one asset GET;
   `bin/chaos` resolves to the served bytes; the smoke test really exec'd the new build
   and never exec'd the old one; `config.toml` keeps `show_tips = false` and the
   user-authored `[mcp_servers.docs]` table while gaining `gh-release`; `sessions/` is
   byte-identical; `version.json` and `installed_on_disk_version()` both say 0.2.7; the
   0.2.5 build is still on disk to fall back to.

2. `an_unverifiable_feed_is_refused_before_its_artifact_runs`
   With `CHAOS_REQUIRE_SIG=1`, a feed that cannot produce a signature this build can
   check is refused. The error names `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY`; the marker file does not
   exist, so the artifact was downloaded and never exec'd; the old build is still active.
   The request count proves the refusal happens after the download and before the exec,
   which is the only ordering that matters here.
   Limit recorded in the test's own doc comment: the public key is baked in at compile
   time (`option_env!`), so no test in this crate can produce an artifact whose signature
   *verifies*. A test build resolves the placeholder key to `SignatureError::NoPublicKey`.
   This case therefore proves the refusal half of the signing gate, not the accept half,
   and the accept half cannot come from this suite at all.
   It was later covered outside it: `scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2
   --all` runs the shipped `signature::verify_file` over all six published artifacts under
   the repository's real public key and accepts every one, refusing a one-byte corruption
   of the same file. See `docs/verification/release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log`.
   What remains uncovered is the combination -- `run_update` with `CHAOS_REQUIRE_SIG=1`
   against a feed whose artifact genuinely verifies -- because no test binary can hold a
   key that accepts anything.

3. `an_interrupted_download_never_becomes_the_active_build`
   The asset server advertises the full length and sends a prefix of it. The update
   returns an error, nothing is staged at `downloads/chaos-0.2.7-<platform>`, the
   artifact was never exec'd, and the previous build still serves.

4. `an_artifact_that_cannot_start_is_discarded_and_the_old_build_stays`
   The feed serves an artifact that exits 1. The smoke test did exec it; the error says
   `downloaded binary failed to run` and `Your current version is unchanged`; the rejected
   file is deleted; the asset was fetched exactly once, i.e. a build that will not start
   is not re-downloaded on the same call.

5. `a_retried_update_succeeds_once_the_feed_recovers`
   The truncating feed fails once, then the same `run_update` (no reset, no `--force`)
   succeeds against a healthy feed and leaves 0.2.7 active with 0.2.5 retained. This is
   the rollback-adjacent guarantee in the direction that is actually implemented: a
   half-finished attempt leaves no state that poisons the next one.

6. `the_post_install_sweep_keeps_rollback_and_clears_old_and_legacy_files`
   Seeded with `chaos-0.2.3`, `grok-0.2.4`, `grok-0.2.2` and a `chaos-0.1.9-x.tmp`
   backdated past `STALE_TMP_AGE`, then updated. After the update: the version you rolled
   back to (0.2.5) is kept, the older `chaos-` build is gone, the newest other-brand build
   is kept, the older one is gone, the stale `.tmp` is gone, a `.tmp` that is still fresh
   survives, and the whole update still fetched the asset exactly once.

== a real gap this found, and the fix in auto_update.rs

Nothing in this crate checked that a download delivered the bytes it advertised. A
truncated body was published as an installed build and only stopped, if at all, by
whatever happened to be in the truncated bytes. The truncating fixture had to be built
with a *runnable prefix* (a valid `#!/bin/sh` program followed by 8 KiB of `#` comments)
before the suite noticed, because a half-cut script usually fails to exec and the smoke
test then hides the hole.

So `auto_update.rs` now has `check_complete_body(written, advertised, url)`, called after
the stream ends in both `download_with_progress` and `download_silent`, and
`download_range` bails when a ranged GET returns fewer bytes than the range asked for --
the parallel path pre-allocates with `set_len`, so a short chunk used to leave a silent
zero hole in the middle of the artifact. `download_silent` did not even read
`content_length()` before.

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-update/src/auto_update_tests.rs::a_short_body_is_rejected_and_a_whole_one_is_not`
unit-tests the new helper directly: a short body and an over-long body are both errors, an
exact body is fine, and a response with no `Content-Length` is not treated as a failure.

== the checks were confirmed to be load-bearing by breaking them

Both mutations were applied to `auto_update.rs`, the suite re-run, and the source restored
(the restored file was confirmed against a pre-edit copy by `diff -u`, which showed only
`check_complete_body`, its two call sites, and the `download_range` byte-count check).

  M1  remove the verify_downloaded_artifact call from install_gh_release
      -> exactly one failure: an_unverifiable_feed_is_refused_before_its_artifact_runs
         (the unsigned artifact was exec'd)

  M2  swallow the stream error and delete the check_complete_body calls
      -> two failures: an_interrupted_download_never_becomes_the_active_build and
         a_retried_update_succeeds_once_the_feed_recovers
            a truncated artifact must fail the update: Some("0.2.7")

M2 is the one that mattered. It survived an earlier version of the test, which is the
reason the fixture is built with a runnable prefix.

== the whole crate, after the fix

  cargo test -p xai-grok-update --offline --locked
  165 unit + 4 blitz (1 stress ignored) + 5 + 10 + 24 + 19 + 7 + 9 + 28 network
  + 8 signature + 28 subprocess + 6 feed e2e, doc-tests 0
  313 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored   (exit 0, no compiler warnings)

The pre-existing blitz truncation matrices in `tests/test_blitz_cancel.rs` still pass with
the new byte-count checks in place; that file's stale comment, which described a
byte-count guard and an error classification that did not exist, has been replaced with
the three guards that are really there. `tests/test_signature_integration.rs` carried a
module doc claiming it drove `verify_downloaded_artifact` against a mock `.sig` server; it
does not -- it calls `signature::verify_file`/`verify_bytes` and the two switches -- so the
header now says that and points here for the pipeline side.

== what this file is NOT evidence about

- The accept half of signature verification *inside this suite* (see case 2's limitation).
  The accept half itself is now evidenced separately, against real published artifacts, in
  `docs/verification/release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log`.
- Anything on Windows: the file is `#![cfg(unix)]` because the artifacts are `/bin/sh`
  scripts. The Windows asset name and the `.exe` rename path are not exercised here.
- A real GitHub release feed or a real CDN. `GROK_INSTALLER=gh-release` is set by the
  tests, so the install-script branch is not what these six cases ran.
  The `install.sh` handoff against the real published release is a different run with its
  own transcript: `docs/verification/install-sh-linux-2026-10-02.log`.
- Old-data migration beyond what case 1 asserts (config merging and `sessions/` bytes).
  On-disk formats from versions this repository no longer builds are not covered.
